(a) In addition to the level-of-approval requirements imposed by section 3-185, formal sealed bids shall be required in the conducting of any procurement with a value equaling or exceeding the value of procurements subject to competitive bidding procedures mandated by Georgia law, unless the procurement is designated as a special improvement category by section 3-189 with that section setting forth the protocols governing the conducting of special procurements.
(b) Advertisements for such procurements shall meet the following requirements.
(1) Advertisements for a sealed bid shall be published for two consecutive weeks in the official organ of Pickens County, with the first advertisement occurring at least two weeks prior to the opening of the bids;
(2) Notice of the opportunity to submit the sealed bid shall be conspicuously posted in the Pickens County Administrative Building at 1266 East Church Street, Jasper, Georgia, during the week of the first aforesaid advertisement;
(3) Advertisements for sealed bids shall be placed in the Georgia Procurement Registry in accordance with Georgia law during the week of the first aforesaid advertisement; and
(4) Advertisements for sealed bids shall be posted on the county's website during the week of the first aforesaid advertisement.
(c) An invitation to submit sealed bids shall be issued for each procurement and shall be provided to any prospective bidder requesting the invitation.
Invitations shall include all specifications and all contractual terms and conditions applicable to the procurement.
(d) A minimum of three vendors will be contacted to submit sealed bids, pending vendor availability.
(e) The openings of sealed bids shall meet the following requirements:
(1) All sealed bids shall have a public bid opening at the date, time and place to appear in the aforesaid advertisements and notices;
(2) The sealed bids shall be recorded and placed into the public record;
(3) All sealed bids shall be opened at the same meeting, with the date and times opened documented in the public record;
(4) Sealed bids shall not be opened without the specified witness;
(5) The witness and person opening the sealed bids shall be documented in the public record; and
(6) The recorded bid sheet and sealed bids shall be provided to the chairperson or the designee of the chairperson, who shall then provide copies of the recorded bid sheet and sealed bids to the board of commissioners at its next regular meeting.
(f) Considering all submitted qualified bids, the procurement shall be awarded to the lowest bid that meets all bid specifications and requirements that is submitted by the bidder who is capable of best delivering the product or service that meets service or product quality standards, who has a favorable history of delivering the product or service and who has favorable references.
(g) The following criteria should be utilized in making an award:
(1) Bid price;
(2) Bidder's experience;
(3) Bidder's reputation;
(4) Bidder's leadership;
(5) Bidder's personnel;
(6) Bidder's facilities;
(7) Bidder's equipment;
(8) Bidder's financial capabilities;
(9) Extensiveness of the bidder's utilization by other state governments and other local governments; and
(10) Product reputation.
Note: Awarding the procurement does not require the acceptance of the lowest evaluated bid price, as many critical factors in addition to cost must be considered in determining whether the county would receive the best value possible from a particular procurement.
(h) If the value of the most favored bid exceeds the budgeted amount for the item for which the bid was submitted, the county may negotiate with such apparent low bidder to obtain a contact price within the budgeted amount, which may involve changes in the scope of work and other bid requirements.
(i) At the discretion of the chairperson, the county may discontinue the sealed-bid process for any particular procurement at any time or may reject any unresponsive bid. Notice of said cancellation shall be sent to all parties submitting bids and shall be posted on the county's website.
(j) Under no circumstances shall any procurement be arbitrarily segmented to circumvent the requirements mandated by this section.
(Ord. of 5-22-2025(2), § 1(Exh. A))

281:
(a) In addition to the level-of-approval requirements imposed by section 3-185, competitive proposals shall be required in the conducting of any procurement with a value ranging from $10,000.00 up to the value of procurements subject to the competitive bidding procedures mandated by Georgia law, unless the procurement is designated as a special improvement category in section 3-189 with that section setting forth the protocols governing the conducting of special procurements.
(b) A minimum of three vendors will be contacted to submit competitive proposals, pending vendor availability.
(c) Proposals shall be submitted in hard copy, email, or via facsimile to the applicable department head(s), who shall forward the proposals to the chairperson and any designees of the chairperson.
(d) The procurement shall be awarded to the proposing vendor whose proposal is expected to provide the most value to the county in light of applicable cost considerations and the operational needs of the county.
(e) Such vendor shall be selected on the basis of the following criteria:
(1) Proposal price;
(2) Proposer's experience;
(3) Proposer's reputation;
(4) Proposer's leadership;
(5) Proposer's personnel;
(6) Proposer's facilities;
(7) Proposer's equipment;
(8) Proposer's financial capabilities;
(9) Extensiveness of the proposer's utilization by other state governments and other local governments; and
(10) Product reputation.
Note: While the amount of the proposal price is an important factor, the acceptance of the lowest evaluated proposal price is not required as many factors in addition to cost must be considered in determining whether the county would receive the best value possible from a particular procurement.
(f) At the discretion of the chairperson, the county may discontinue the procurement process at any time or may reject any unresponsive proposal.
Notice of cancellation shall be sent to all parties submitting proposals and shall be posted on the county's website.
(g) Procurements ranging from $40,000.00 up to the value of procurements subject to the competitive bidding procedures mandated by Georgia law shall be subject to the following requirements for advertisements and notices:
(1) Advertisements shall be published for two consecutive weeks in the official organ of Pickens County, with the first advertisement occurring at least two weeks prior to the opening of the proposals;
(2) Notice of the proposal opportunity shall be conspicuously posted in the Pickens County Administrative Building during the week of the first aforesaid advertisement;
(3) Advertisements shall be placed in the Georgia Procurement Registry during the week of the first aforesaid advertisement; and
(4) Advertisements shall be posted on the county's website during the week of the first aforesaid advertisement.
(h) Under no circumstances shall any procurement be arbitrarily segmented to circumvent the requirements mandated by this section.
(Ord. of 5-22-2025(2), § 1(Exh. A))
